A||N||4||Monkeyman||A/C belt squeals--How to tighten??||||5||
Z||000000||Monkeyman||06-10-2001||12:12 PM||||I kicked my A/C on yesterday and the darn thing squeals like a pig.  Very embarrassing and I'm sure it's not good for either the motor or the compressor.  How do I tighten the belt?  Can it be done with one person or do I need help?  What about replacing the belt?  I recently got a TON of oil on the belt (been washed off, but probably still has oil embedded in it) if it matters.  Also, the alternator belt started squealing just after I washed her last night.  It stopped as soon as the belt dried, but I might need to tighten this up, too.<P>Can someone help me out, please??<P>MM<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||theogre||06-10-2001||12:30 PM||||Just replace the belts. Once the've squeeled under a load like that they are shot. The alternator one is probably just as contaminated as the AC. Clean the pulleys with brake parts cleaner before you install the new belt.<P>You have to do it from under the car. there are several bolts you have to loosen. Basically every bolt on the compressor has to come loose.<P>Various people have indicated that the belt tightening tool that looks like a very big hook makes this job much easier. You use the hook to grab the compressor ears then rotate the whole mess with a big socket handle.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 06-10-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||sjp777||06-10-2001||12:31 PM||||Jack up the pass side, crawl your ass under there, loosen the adjustment and pivot bolts and use whatever you can to get behind the compressor and move it away from the block before re-tightening.<P>Not rocket science. Although there will probably be a lot of sweating, swearing and getting dirty involved<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/890sm_4.jpg"> <BR>88GT 5sp, in desperate need of an LT1<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero88gt1/</A>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Monkeyman||06-10-2001||12:33 PM||||Thanx, ogre.  I might have the shop do this.  Seems like a big job.<P>MM||||reg||1
Z||000004||Steve Normington||06-11-2001||01:28 PM||||I've been able to replace my AC compressor belt on my 2.8 without loosening the upper bolts.<P>Jack up back end as far as possible (safety first).<BR>Remove the metal shield under the compressor.<BR>Loosen the lower bolt.<BR>Pry the bolt mounting point with a big screwdriver through the three holes in the bottom of the bracket.<BR>While still prying, tighten the bolt.<BR>Replace the metal shield.<P>Note: The PO had installed a short bolt that had partially stripped the threads in the compressor. I had to clean them up with a tap and install a longer bolt to keep the compressor in place.||||reg||1
